    {
      "id": "sol.amc-10a-2019-p2.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Both \\(20!\\) and \\(15!\\) contain at least three factors of \\(5\\) and three factors of \\(2\\), so both are divisible by \\(2^3\\cdot5^3=1000.\\)\n\nTheir difference is therefore also divisible by \\(1000.\\)\n\nBeing a multiple of \\(1000\\) makes the last three digits \\(0,\\) which shows that the hundreds digit is also \\(0.\\)\n\nThus, A is the correct answer.",
      "hints": [
        "Look only at divisibility by \\(1000\\)",
        "Both factorials contain at least three factors of \\(5\\) and three factors of \\(2\\)"
      ],
